#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5760a53a-1a0c-568e-ac9c-c820eaad3010" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Formula for the Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(-3)^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(-3)^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559558} :answer-field/id #uuid "002196b5-3d0b-53c5-b861-202ffed83ecb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15178543-fce2-524a-bf78-a8e6e6bf0c75" :question/math-academy-id "q-231755" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $4$th term is $-135$ and the common ratio is $-3.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $4$th term is ${a}_{4}=-135,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{4-1} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{3} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-27) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5(-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d82eb202-fec9-5769-a006-67d42b932303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559345} :answer-field/id #uuid "1381eca8-6c85-572d-a31b-e3bf882f85e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6635ce75-68d9-56d3-9e68-8210fcf2e22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-231757" :question/problem "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the eighth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5760a53a-1a0c-568e-ac9c-c820eaad3010" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Formula for the Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70077891-86db-502d-895f-ded2194be2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559293} {:answer/id #uuid "72e91449-71dc-56a0-be51-67be87f1ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559304} {:answer/id #uuid "73e92523-fc3e-578c-9d40-6df5fc8e63ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=1+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559306} {:answer/id #uuid "b3782bf8-ceaf-5b27-82d4-96d98b8fc40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559465} {:answer/id #uuid "b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559470} :answer-field/id #uuid "27573e10-1873-5b44-a68a-7f1ad53ececa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" :question/math-academy-id "q-5437" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{9}{4},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
